دِ ", "translation": "And the Horn will be blown. That is the Day of [carrying out] the threat.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَنُفِخَ", "translit": "َhkِfُnَw", "root": "نفخ"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلصُّورِ", "translit": "ِrwُّṣlٱ", "root": "صور"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ذَٰلِكَ", "translit": "َkِlَٰhd", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "يَوْمُ", "translit": "ُmْwَy", "root": "يوم"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ٱلْوَعِيدِ", "translit": "ِdyِʿعَwْlٱ", "root": "وعد"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 21, "text": "وَجَآءَتْ كُلُّ نَفْسٍ مَّعَهَا سَآئِقٌ وَشَهِيدٌ ", "translation": "And every soul will come, with it a driver and a witness.[1]", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَجَآءَتْ", "translit": "ْtَءٓāَjgَw", "root": "جيا"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "كُلُّ", "translit": "ُّlُk", "root": "كلل"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "نَفْسٍ", "translit": "ٍsْfَn", "root": "نفس"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "مَّعَهَا", "translit": "āَhَʿعَّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "سَآئِقٌ", "translit": "ٌqِyʾٓāَs", "root": "سوق"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَشَهِيدٌ", "translit": "ٌdyِhَhsَw", "root": "شهد"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 22, "text": "لَّقَدْ كُنتَ فِى غَفْلَةٍ مِّنْ هَٰذَا فَكَشَفْنَا عَنكَ غِطَآءَكَ فَبَصَرُكَ ٱلْيَوْمَ حَدِيدٌ ", "translation": "[It will be said], \"You were certainly in unmindfulness of this, and We have removed from you your cover,[1] so your sight, this Day, is sharp.\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "لَّقَدْ", "translit": "ْdَqَّl", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "كُنتَ", "translit": "َtnُk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "غَفْلَةٍ", "translit": "ٍẗَlْfَغ", "root": "غفل"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "مِّنْ", "translit": "ْnِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "هَٰذَا", "translit": "āَhdَٰh", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "فَكَشَفْنَا", "translit": "āَnْfَhsَkَf", "root": "كشف"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "عَنكَ", "translit": "َknَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "غِطَآءَكَ", "translit": "َkَءٓāَṭِغ", "root": "غطو"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "فَبَصَرُكَ", "translit": "َkُrَṣَbَf", "root": "بصر"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "ٱلْيَوْمَ", "translit": "َmْwَyْlٱ", "root": "يوم"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "حَدِيدٌ", "translit": "ٌdyِdَḥ", "root": "حدد"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 23, "text": "وَقَالَ قَرِينُهُۥ هَٰذَا مَا لَدَىَّ عَتِيدٌ ", "translation": "And his companion, [the angel], will say, \"This [record] is what is with me, prepared.\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَقَالَ", "translit": "َlāَqَw", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "قَرِينُهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhُnyِrَq", "root": "قرن"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "هَٰذَا", "translit": "āَhdَٰh", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "لَدَىَّ", "translit": "َّàَdَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "عَتِيدٌ", "translit": "ٌdyِtَʿع", "root": "عتد"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 24, "text": "أَلْقِيَا فِى جَهَنَّمَ كُلَّ كَفَّارٍ عَنِيدٍ ", "translation": "[Allāh will say], \"Throw into Hell every obstinate disbeliever,", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "أَلْقِيَا", "translit": "āَyِqْlَaʾ", "root": "لقي"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "جَهَنَّمَ", "translit": "َmَّnَhَjg", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "كُلَّ", "translit": "َّlُk", "root": "كلل"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "كَفَّارٍ", "translit": "ٍrāَّfَk", "root": "كفر"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "عَنِيدٍ", "translit": "ٍdyِnَʿع", "root": "عند"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 25, "text": "مَّنَّاعٍ لِّلْخَيْرِ مُعْتَدٍ مُّرِيبٍ ", "translation": "Preventer of good, aggressor, and doubter,", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "مَّنَّاعٍ", "translit": "ٍʿعāَّnَّm", "root": "منع"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "لِّلْخَيْرِ", "translit": "ِrْyَhkْlِّl", "root": "خير"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مُعْتَدٍ", "translit": "ٍdَtْʿعُm", "root": "عدو"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "مُّرِيبٍ", "translit": "ٍbyِrُّm", "root": "ريب"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 26, "text": "ٱلَّذِى جَعَلَ مَعَ ٱللَّهِ إِلَٰهًا ءَاخَرَ فَأَلْقِيَاهُ فِى ٱلْعَذَابِ ٱلشَّدِيدِ ", "translation": "Who made [as equal] with Allāh another deity; then throw him into the severe punishment.\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "ٱلَّذِى", "translit": "àِhdَّlٱ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "جَعَلَ", "translit": "َlَʿعَjg", "root": "جعل"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مَعَ", "translit": "َʿعَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱللَّهِ", "translit": "ِhَّllٱ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "إِلَٰهًا", "translit": "āًhَٰlِiʾ", "root": "اله"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ءَاخَرَ", "translit": "َrَhkāَء", "root": "اخر"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "فَأَلْقِيَاهُ", "translit": "ُhāَyِqْlَaʾَf", "root": "لقي"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ٱلْعَذَابِ", "translit": "ِbāَhdَʿعْlٱ", "root": "عذب"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "ٱلشَّدِيدِ", "translit": "ِdyِdَّhslٱ", "root": "شدد"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 27, "text": "قَالَ قَرِينُهُۥ رَبَّنَا مَآ أَطْغَيْتُهُۥ وَلَٰكِن كَانَ فِى ضَلَٰلٍۭ بَعِيدٍ ", "translation": "His [devil] companion will say, \"Our Lord, I did not make him transgress, but he [himself] was in extreme error.\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "قَالَ", "translit": "َlāَq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "قَرِينُهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhُnyِrَq", "root": "قرن"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "رَبَّنَا", "translit": "āَnَّbَr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "مَآ", "translit": "ٓāَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "أَطْغَيْتُهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhُtْyَغْṭَaʾ", "root": "طغي"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَلَٰكِن", "translit": "nِkَٰlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "كَانَ", "translit": "َnāَk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "ضَلَٰلٍۭ", "translit": "ٍۭlَٰlَḍ", "root": "ضلل"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "بَعِيدٍ", "translit": "ٍdyِʿعَb", "root": "بعد"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 28, "text": "قَالَ لَا تَخْتَصِمُوا۟ لَدَىَّ وَقَدْ قَدَّمْتُ إِلَيْكُم بِٱلْوَعِيدِ ", "translation": "[Allāh] will say, \"Do not dispute before Me, while I had already presented to you the threat [i.e., warning].", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "قَالَ", "translit": "َlāَq", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "لَا", "translit": "āَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "تَخْتَصِمُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُmِṣَtْhkَt", "root": "خصم"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "لَدَىَّ", "translit": "َّàَdَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَقَدْ", "translit": "ْdَqَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "قَدَّمْتُ", "translit": "ُtْmَّdَq", "root": "قدم"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "إِلَيْكُم", "translit": "mُkْyَlِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "بِٱلْوَعِيدِ", "translit": "ِdyِʿعَwْlٱِb", "root": "وعد"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 29, "text": "مَا يُبَدَّلُ ٱلْقَوْلُ لَدَىَّ وَمَآ أَنَا۠ بِظَلَّٰمٍ لِّلْعَبِيدِ ", "translation": "The word [i.e., decree] will not be changed with Me, and never will I be unjust to the servants.\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "يُبَدَّلُ", "translit": "ُlَّdَbُy", "root": "بدل"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلْقَوْلُ", "translit": "ُlْwَqْlٱ", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "لَدَىَّ", "translit": "َّàَdَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَمَآ", "translit": "ٓāَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "أَنَا۠", "translit": "۠āَnَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "بِظَلَّٰمٍ", "translit": "ٍmَّٰlَẓِb", "root": "ظلم"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "لِّلْعَبِيدِ", "translit": "ِdyِbَʿعْlِّl", "root": "عبد"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 30, "text": "يَوْمَ نَقُولُ لِجَهَنَّمَ هَلِ ٱمْتَلَأْتِ وَتَقُولُ هَلْ مِن مَّزِيدٍ ", "translation": "On the Day We will say to Hell, \"Have you been filled?\" and it will say, \"Are there some more,\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "يَوْمَ", "translit": "َmْwَy", "root": "يوم"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "نَقُولُ", "translit": "ُlwُqَn", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "لِجَهَنَّمَ", "translit": "َmَّnَhَjgِl", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "هَلِ", "translit": "ِlَh", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱمْتَلَأْتِ", "translit": "ِtْaʾَlَtْmٱ", "root": "ملا"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "وَتَقُولُ", "translit": "ُlwُqَtَw", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "هَلْ", "translit": "ْlَh", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "مَّزِيدٍ", "translit": "ٍdyِzَّm", "root": "زيد"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 31, "text": "وَأُزْلِفَتِ ٱلْجَنَّةُ لِلْمُتَّقِينَ غَيْرَ بَعِيدٍ ", "translation": "And Paradise will be brought near to the righteous, not far,", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَأُزْلِفَتِ", "translit": "ِtَfِlْzُaʾَw", "root": "زلف"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلْجَنَّةُ", "translit": "ُẗَّnَjgْlٱ", "root": "جنن"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "لِلْمُتَّقِينَ", "translit": "َnyِqَّtُmْlِl", "root": "وقي"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "غَيْرَ", "translit": "َrْyَغ", "root": "غير"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "بَعِيدٍ", "translit": "ٍdyِʿعَb", "root": "بعد"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 32, "text": "هَٰذَا مَا تُوعَدُونَ لِكُلِّ أَوَّابٍ حَفِيظٍ ", "translation": "[It will be said], \"This is what you were promised - for every returner [to Allāh] and keeper [of His covenant].", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "هَٰذَا", "translit": "āَhdَٰh", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "تُوعَدُونَ", "translit": "َnwُdَʿعwُt", "root": "وعد"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "لِكُلِّ", "translit": "ِّlُkِl", "root": "كلل"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "أَوَّابٍ", "translit": "ٍbāَّwَaʾ", "root": "اوب"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "حَفِيظٍ", "translit": "ٍẓyِfَḥ", "root": "حفظ"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 33, "text": "مَّنْ خَشِىَ ٱلرَّحْمَٰنَ بِٱلْغَيْبِ وَجَآءَ بِقَلْبٍ مُّنِيبٍ ", "translation": "Who feared the Most Merciful in the unseen and came with a heart returning [in repentance].", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "مَّنْ", "translit": "ْnَّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "خَشِىَ", "translit": "َàِhsَhk", "root": "خشي"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلرَّحْمَٰنَ", "translit": "َnَٰmْḥَّrlٱ", "root": "رحم"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "بِٱلْغَيْبِ", "translit": "ِbْyَغْlٱِb", "root": "غيب"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَجَآءَ", "translit": "َءٓāَjgَw", "root": "جيا"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "بِقَلْبٍ", "translit": "ٍbْlَqِb", "root": "قلب"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "مُّنِيبٍ", "translit": "ٍbyِnُّm", "root": "نوب"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 34, "text": "ٱدْخُلُوهَا بِسَلَٰمٍۖ ذَٰلِكَ يَوْمُ ٱلْخُلُودِ ", "translation": "Enter it in peace. This is the Day of Eternity.\"", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "ٱدْخُلُوهَا", "translit": "āَhwُlُhkْdٱ", "root": "دخل"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "بِسَلَٰمٍ", "translit": "ٍmَٰlَsِb", "root": "سلم"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ذَٰلِكَ", "translit": "َkِlَٰhd", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "يَوْمُ", "translit": "ُmْwَy", "root": "يوم"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱلْخُلُودِ", "translit": "ِdwُlُhkْlٱ", "root": "خلد"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 35, "text": "لَهُم مَّا يَشَآءُونَ فِيهَا وَلَدَيْنَا مَزِيدٌ ", "translation": "They will have whatever they wish therein, and with Us is more.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "لَهُم", "translit": "mُhَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "مَّا", "translit": "āَّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "يَشَآءُونَ", "translit": "َnwُءٓāَhsَy", "root": "شيا"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "فِيهَا", "translit": "āَhyِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَلَدَيْنَا", "translit": "āَnْyَdَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "مَزِيدٌ", "translit": "ٌdyِzَm", "root": "زيد"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 36, "text": "وَكَمْ أَهْلَكْنَا قَبْلَهُم مِّن قَرْنٍ هُمْ أَشَدُّ مِنْهُم بَطْشًا فَنَقَّبُوا۟ فِى ٱلْبِلَٰدِ هَلْ مِن مَّحِيصٍ ", "translation": "And how many a generation before them did We destroy who were greater than them in [striking] power and had explored throughout the lands. Is there any place of escape?", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَكَمْ", "translit": "ْmَkَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أَهْلَكْنَا", "translit": "āَnْkَlْhَaʾ", "root": "هلك"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "قَبْلَهُم", "translit": "mُhَlْbَq", "root": "قبل"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "مِّن", "translit": "nِّm", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "قَرْنٍ", "translit": "ٍnْrَq", "root": "قرن"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "هُمْ", "translit": "ْmُh", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "أَشَدُّ", "translit": "ُّdَhsَaʾ", "root": "شدد"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "مِنْهُم", "translit": "mُhْnِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "بَطْشًا", "translit": "āًhsْṭَb", "root": "بطش"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "فَنَقَّبُوا۟", "translit": "۟āwُbَّqَnَf", "root": "نقب"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "ٱلْبِلَٰدِ", "translit": "ِdَٰlِbْlٱ", "root": "بلد"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "هَلْ", "translit": "ْlَh", "root": ""}, {"id": 14, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 15, "arabic": "مَّحِيصٍ", "translit": "ٍṣyِḥَّm", "root": "حيص"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 37, "text": "إِنَّ فِى ذَٰلِكَ لَذِكْرَىٰ لِمَن كَانَ لَهُۥ قَلْبٌ أَوْ أَلْقَى ٱلسَّمْعَ وَهُوَ شَهِيدٌ ", "translation": "Indeed in that is a reminder for whoever has a heart or who listens while he is present [in mind].", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "إِنَّ", "translit": "َّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ذَٰلِكَ", "translit": "َkِlَٰhd", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "لَذِكْرَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَrْkِhdَl", "root": "ذكر"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "لِمَن", "translit": "nَmِl", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "كَانَ", "translit": "َnāَk", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "لَهُۥ", "translit": "ۥُhَl", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "قَلْبٌ", "translit": "ٌbْlَq", "root": "قلب"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "أَوْ", "translit": "ْwَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "أَلْقَى", "translit": "àَqْlَaʾ", "root": "لقي"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "ٱلسَّمْعَ", "translit": "َʿعْmَّslٱ", "root": "سمع"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "وَهُوَ", "translit": "َwُhَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "شَهِيدٌ", "translit": "ٌdyِhَhs", "root": "شهد"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 38, "text": "وَلَقَدْ خَلَقْنَا ٱلسَّمَٰوَٰتِ وَٱلْأَرْضَ وَمَا بَيْنَهُمَا فِى سِتَّةِ أَيَّامٍ وَمَا مَسَّنَا مِن لُّغُوبٍ ", "translation": "And We did certainly create the heavens and earth and what is between them in six days, and there touched Us no weariness.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَلَقَدْ", "translit": "ْdَqَlَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "خَلَقْنَا", "translit": "āَnْqَlَhk", "root": "خلق"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلسَّمَٰوَٰتِ", "translit": "ِtَٰwَٰmَّslٱ", "root": "سمو"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "وَٱلْأَرْضَ", "translit": "َḍْrَaʾْlٱَw", "root": "ارض"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَمَا", "translit": "āَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "بَيْنَهُمَا", "translit": "āَmُhَnْyَb", "root": "بين"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "فِى", "translit": "àِf", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "سِتَّةِ", "translit": "ِẗَّtِs", "root": "ستت"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "أَيَّامٍ", "translit": "ٍmāَّyَaʾ", "root": "يوم"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "وَمَا", "translit": "āَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "مَسَّنَا", "translit": "āَnَّsَm", "root": "مسس"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "لُّغُوبٍ", "translit": "ٍbwُغُّl", "root": "لغب"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 39, "text": "فَٱصْبِرْ عَلَىٰ مَا يَقُولُونَ وَسَبِّحْ بِحَمْدِ رَبِّكَ قَبْلَ طُلُوعِ ٱلشَّمْسِ وَقَبْلَ ٱلْغُرُوبِ ", "translation": "So be patient, [O Muḥammad], over what they say and exalt [Allāh] with praise of your Lord before the rising of the sun and before its setting,", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "فَٱصْبِرْ", "translit": "ْrِbْṣٱَf", "root": "صبر"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "عَلَىٰ", "translit": "ٰàَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "مَا", "translit": "āَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "يَقُولُونَ", "translit": "َnwُlwُqَy", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَسَبِّحْ", "translit": "ْḥِّbَsَw", "root": "سبح"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "بِحَمْدِ", "translit": "ِdْmَḥِb", "root": "حمد"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "رَبِّكَ", "translit": "َkِّbَr", "root": "ربب"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "قَبْلَ", "translit": "َlْbَq", "root": "قبل"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "طُلُوعِ", "translit": "ِʿعwُlُṭ", "root": "طلع"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "ٱلشَّمْسِ", "translit": "ِsْmَّhslٱ", "root": "شمس"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "وَقَبْلَ", "translit": "َlْbَqَw", "root": "قبل"}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "ٱلْغُرُوبِ", "translit": "ِbwُrُغْlٱ", "root": "غرب"}], "hasIhya": true}, {"ayah": 40, "text": "وَمِنَ ٱلَّيْلِ فَسَبِّحْهُ وَأَدْبَٰرَ ٱلسُّجُودِ ", "translation": "And [in part] of the night exalt Him and after prostration [i.e., prayer].", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَمِنَ", "translit": "َnِmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "ٱلَّيْلِ", "translit": "ِlْyَّlٱ", "root": "ليل"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "فَسَبِّحْهُ", "translit": "ُhْḥِّbَsَf", "root": "سبح"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "وَأَدْبَٰرَ", "translit": "َrَٰbْdَaʾَw", "root": "دبر"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ٱلسُّجُودِ", "translit": "ِdwُjgُّslٱ", "root": "سجد"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 41, "text": "وَٱسْتَمِعْ يَوْمَ يُنَادِ ٱلْمُنَادِ مِن مَّكَانٍ قَرِيبٍ ", "translation": "And listen on the Day when the Caller[1] will call out from a place that is near -", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "وَٱسْتَمِعْ", "translit": "ْʿعِmَtْsٱَw", "root": "سمع"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "يَوْمَ", "translit": "َmْwَy", "root": "يوم"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "يُنَادِ", "translit": "ِdāَnُy", "root": "ندي"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "ٱلْمُنَادِ", "translit": "ِdāَnُmْlٱ", "root": "ندي"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "مِن", "translit": "nِm", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "مَّكَانٍ", "translit": "ٍnāَkَّm", "root": "كون"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "قَرِيبٍ", "translit": "ٍbyِrَq", "root": "قرب"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 42, "text": "يَوْمَ يَسْمَعُونَ ٱلصَّيْحَةَ بِٱلْحَقِّۚ ذَٰلِكَ يَوْمُ ٱلْخُرُوجِ ", "translation": "The Day they will hear the blast [of the Horn] in truth. That is the Day of Emergence [from the graves].", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "يَوْمَ", "translit": "َmْwَy", "root": "يوم"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "يَسْمَعُونَ", "translit": "َnwُʿعَmْsَy", "root": "سمع"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلصَّيْحَةَ", "translit": "َẗَḥْyَّṣlٱ", "root": "صيح"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "بِٱلْحَقِّ", "translit": "ِّqَḥْlٱِb", "root": "حقق"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "ذَٰلِكَ", "translit": "َkِlَٰhd", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "يَوْمُ", "translit": "ُmْwَy", "root": "يوم"}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "ٱلْخُرُوجِ", "translit": "ِjgwُrُhkْlٱ", "root": "خرج"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 43, "text": "إِنَّا نَحْنُ نُحْىِۦ وَنُمِيتُ وَإِلَيْنَا ٱلْمَصِيرُ ", "translation": "Indeed, it is We who give life and cause death, and to Us is the destination", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "إِنَّا", "translit": "āَّnِiʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "نَحْنُ", "translit": "ُnْḥَn", "root": ""}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "نُحْىِۦ", "translit": "ۦِàْḥُn", "root": "حيي"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "وَنُمِيتُ", "translit": "ُtyِmُnَw", "root": "موت"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَإِلَيْنَا", "translit": "āَnْyَlِiʾَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ٱلْمَصِيرُ", "translit": "ُryِṣَmْlٱ", "root": "صير"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 44, "text": "يَوْمَ تَشَقَّقُ ٱلْأَرْضُ عَنْهُمْ سِرَاعًاۚ ذَٰلِكَ حَشْرٌ عَلَيْنَا يَسِيرٌ ", "translation": "On the Day the earth breaks away from them [and they emerge] rapidly; that is a gathering easy for Us.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "يَوْمَ", "translit": "َmْwَy", "root": "يوم"}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "تَشَقَّقُ", "translit": "ُqَّqَhsَt", "root": "شقق"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "ٱلْأَرْضُ", "translit": "ُḍْrَaʾْlٱ", "root": "ارض"}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "عَنْهُمْ", "translit": "ْmُhْnَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "سِرَاعًا", "translit": "āًʿعāَrِs", "root": "سرع"}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "ذَٰلِكَ", "translit": "َkِlَٰhd", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "حَشْرٌ", "translit": "ٌrْhsَḥ", "root": "حشر"}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "عَلَيْنَا", "translit": "āَnْyَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "يَسِيرٌ", "translit": "ٌryِsَy", "root": "يسر"}], "hasIhya": false}, {"ayah": 45, "text": "نَّحْنُ أَعْلَمُ بِمَا يَقُولُونَۖ وَمَآ أَنتَ عَلَيْهِم بِجَبَّارٍۖ فَذَكِّرْ بِٱلْقُرْءَانِ مَن يَخَافُ وَعِيدِ ", "translation": "We are most knowing of what they say, and you are not over them a tyrant.[1] But remind by the Qur’ān whoever fears My threat.", "words": [{"id": 1, "arabic": "نَّحْنُ", "translit": "ُnْḥَّn", "root": ""}, {"id": 2, "arabic": "أَعْلَمُ", "translit": "ُmَlْʿعَaʾ", "root": "علم"}, {"id": 3, "arabic": "بِمَا", "translit": "āَmِb", "root": ""}, {"id": 4, "arabic": "يَقُولُونَ", "translit": "َnwُlwُqَy", "root": "قول"}, {"id": 5, "arabic": "وَمَآ", "translit": "ٓāَmَw", "root": ""}, {"id": 6, "arabic": "أَنتَ", "translit": "َtnَaʾ", "root": ""}, {"id": 7, "arabic": "عَلَيْهِم", "translit": "mِhْyَlَʿع", "root": ""}, {"id": 8, "arabic": "بِجَبَّارٍ", "translit": "ٍrāَّbَjgِb", "root": "جبر"}, {"id": 9, "arabic": "فَذَكِّرْ", "translit": "ْrِّkَhdَf", "root": "ذكر"}, {"id": 10, "arabic": "بِٱلْقُرْءَانِ", "translit": "ِnāَءْrُqْlٱِb", "root": "قرا"}, {"id": 11, "arabic": "مَن", "translit": "nَm", "root": ""}, {"id": 12, "arabic": "يَخَافُ", "translit": "ُfāَhkَy", "root": "خوف"}, {"id": 13, "arabic": "وَعِيدِ", "translit": "ِdyِʿعَw", "root": "وعد"}], "hasIhya": false}], "tafsir": {"8": [{"arabic": "وقد شرط الله عز وجل الإنابة في الفهم والتذكير فقال تعالى {تبصرة وذكرى لكل عبد منيب} وقال عز وجل {وما يتذكر إلا من ينيب} وقال تعالى {إنما يتذكر أولوا الألباب} فالذي آثر غرور الدنيا على نعيم الآخرة فليس من ذوي الألباب ولذلك لا تنكشف له أسرار الكتاب رابعها أن يكون قد قرأ تفسيرا ظاهرا واعتقد أنه لا معنى لكلمات القرآن إلا ما تناوله النقل عن ابن عباس ومجاهد وغيرهما وأن ما وراء ذلك تفسير بالرأي وأن من فسر القرآن برأيه فقد تبوأ مقعده من النار فهذا أيضا من الحجب العظيمة وسنبين معنى التفسير بالرأي في الباب الرابع وأن ذلك لا يناقض", "english": "I say: These meanings are buried treasures that are not unveiled except to the divinely aided ones. To this 'Ali — may God be pleased with him — alluded by his saying: \"The Messenger of God — may God's peace and blessings be upon him — did not confide to me anything that he concealed from the people, except that God — mighty and majestic — grants to a servant understanding (fahm) of His Book. So let one be eager in seeking that understanding.\" (1) And Ibn Mas'ud — may God be pleased with him — said: \"Whoever desires the knowledge of the first and the last, let him stir up the Qur'an.\" And the greatest of the sciences of the Qur'an lie beneath the names of God — mighty and majestic — and His attributes, for most of creation have grasped from them only matters befitting their understandings and have not stumbled upon their depths.", "book_title": "Etiquette of Quran Recitation", "section_title_ar": "Section 228", "section_title_en": "The Revival of the Religious Sciences — Section 228: On the Inner Meanings of the Qur'an and the Removal of Obstacles to Understanding", "section_index": 228, "anchors": "- Root: فهم (f-h-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Comprehension, discernment, the faculty by which one grasps meanings\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الفهم: هيئة للإنسان بها يتحقق معاني ما يحسن\" — a disposition by which one verifies the realities of what is perceived; distinct from mere hearing or transmission\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: swift apprehension; Metaphorical: the divine gift of insight into hidden meanings, as in the verse {ففهمناها سليمان}\n- Root: قلب (q-l-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Turning, overturning, transformation from one state to another\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"قلب الشيء: تصريفه وصرفه عن وجه إلى وجه\" — the heart as that which is perpetually turned and overturned between states\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: physical turning; Metaphorical: the heart as the seat of understanding that is veiled or illuminated, rusted or polished\n- Root: سمع (s-m-')\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Hearing, the auditory faculty\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"السمع: قوة في الأذن به يدرك الأصوات\" — extends metaphorically to comprehension and obedience; \"أو ألقى السمع وهو شهيد\" — one who casts his hearing while present\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: auditory perception; Metaphorical: attentive obedience and receptive understanding\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد (Restriction & Specification) — governing the precise delimitation of predicates such as \"إنما يتذكر أولوا الألباب\" where restriction confines the action to its proper subject", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Etiquette of Quran Recitation'", "badge": "Insight"}], "16": [{"arabic": "البعد حجابا فإنه أقرب إليك من حبل الوريد وليس هو في مكان حتى تكون السماوات والأرض حجابا بينك وبينه فلا حجاب بينك وبينه إلا شغلك بغيره وشغلك نفسك وشهواتك شغل بغيره وأنت لا تزال مشغولا بنفسك وبشهوات نفسك فكذلك لا تزال محجوبا عنه فالمشغول بحب نفسه مشغول عن الله تعالى والمشغول ببغض نفسه أيضا مشغول عن الله تعالى بكل ما سوى الله مثاله مثال الرقيب الحاضر في مجلس يجمع العاشق والمعشوق فإن التفت قلب العاشق إلى الرقيب وإلى بغضه واستثقاله وكراهة حضوره فهو في حال اشتغال قلبه ببغضه مصروف عن التلذذ ب", "english": "Beyond these five conditions there is a state that is higher than asceticism, namely: that wealth's existence and its loss become equal in his sight. If he finds it, he does not rejoice over it, nor is he harmed [by its presence]; and if he loses it, [his state] is likewise. Rather, his condition is like the condition of ʿĀʾishah — may God Most High be pleased with her — when one hundred thousand dirhams of [state] stipend reached her. She took it and distributed it all that very day. Her maidservant said to her: \"You were not able, in what you distributed today, to buy us even one dirham's worth of meat to break our fast with.\" She replied: \"Had you reminded me, I would have done so.\"", "book_title": "Poverty and Abstinence", "section_title_ar": "Section 1067", "section_title_en": "The Fifth State: The Truly Independent One (Al-Mustaghni) — Beyond Asceticism", "section_index": 1067, "anchors": "- Root: غني (gh-n-y)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of freedom from need, sufficiency, and wealth; the opposite of faqr (poverty/need).\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Ghina is of three grades: (1) absolute freedom from all needs — belonging to God alone {«إن الله لهو الغني الحميد»}; (2) fewness of needs — the praiseworthy richness of the soul (ghina al-nafs); (3) abundance of possessions.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): \"لي عن هذا غنية\" (I have no need of this). Metaphorical (Majaz): \"تغنته القيود\" (fetters made him independent of movement) — here Ghazali extends this majaz to describe one independent of both possession and loss.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Restriction and specification via the construction \"ليس... بل\" (negation followed by corrective reassertion) — used to progressively refine the semantic boundary of the term \"ghani\" versus \"mustaghni.\"", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Poverty and Abstinence'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "ولقد خلقنا الإنسان ونعلم ماتوسوس به نفسه ذكر ذلك تحذيرا وتنبيها للاحتراز منه في المستقبل وروى عبادة بن الصامت أنه عليه السلام قال لرجل سأله أن يوصيه ويعظه إذا أردت أمرا فتدبر عاقبته فإن كان رشدا فامضه وإن كان غيا فانته عنه (1) وقال بعض الحكماء إذا أردت أن يكون العقل غالبا للهوى فلا تعمل بقضاءالشهوة حتى تنظر العاقبة فإن مكث الندامة في القلب أكثر من مكث خفة الشهوة وقال لقمان إن المؤمن إذا أبصر العاقبة أمن الندامة وروى شداد بن أوس عنه صلى الله عليه وسلم أنه قال الكيس من دان", "english": "And some have said: \"Grant that the wrongdoer has been pardoned—has he not still missed the reward of the righteous?\"—pointing thereby to the loss and regret. And God Most High says: {«يَوْمَ يَجْمَعُكُمْ لِيَوْمِ الْجَمْعِ ذَٰلِكَ يَوْمُ التَّغَابُنِ»} (\"The Day He will gather you for the Day of Gathering—that is the Day of Mutual Loss\") (Al-Taghābun 64:9). So this is his counsel to his soul concerning his hours.", "book_title": "Monitoring and Accounting", "section_title_ar": "Section 1232", "section_title_en": "The Revival of the Religious Sciences — Section 1232: The Soul's Covenant with Itself and Reckoning Before Deeds", "section_index": 1232, "anchors": "- Root: نفس (n-f-s)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The self, soul, or spirit; that which breathes and animates; the essence of a thing.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Al-nafs denotes the rūḥ (spirit) and, in theological contexts, the very dhāt (essence/self) of a being—as in {«وَيُحَذِّرُكُمُ اللَّهُ نَفْسَهُ»} (Āl ʿImrān 3:28), where \"His self\" means His essence, not a separate entity.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah): the soul as the seat of desire and action. Metaphorical (majāz): \"دفق نفسه\" (he poured out his self = his blood); the soul as a recalcitrant entity requiring discipline.\n- Root: يوم (y-w-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: A day, from sunrise to sunset; also any span of time, especially the Day of Reckoning.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Yawm signifies the period of the sun's course, but metaphorically extends to any era—most critically \"the Day of Gathering\" (yawm al-jamʿ) and \"the Day of Mutual Loss\" (yawm al-taghābun), where God's attribution of the day to Himself signals its supreme gravity.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: a 24-hour cycle. Metaphorical: \"أيام الله\" (the days of God) = His punitive acts against the disbelievers, as in {«وَذَكِّرْهُمْ بِأَيَّامِ اللَّهِ»} (Ibrāhīm 14:5).\n- Root: حسب (ḥ-s-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To count, reckon, or compute; also to suffice.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Ḥisāb is the employment of number; the Day of Judgment is the day of reckoning where deeds are weighed. The \"ḥusbān\" (computation) of the sun and moon (Al-Anʿām 6:96) marks time for human accountability.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: counting wealth or deeds. Metaphorical: \"احتسبت ما عند فلان\" (I probed what so-and-so possesses) = testing and examining, as the soul must examine its own intentions and acts before God's scrutiny.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — The text uses conditional and imperative constructions (e.g., \"فإياك ثم إياك\" and \"ليشترط على نفسه\") to impose binding covenants, where the predicate restricts the soul's scope of action, demanding a precise, self-imposed legal obligation.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Monitoring and Accounting'", "badge": "Insight"}], "17": [{"arabic": "أتنكرون أن عليكم حافظين كراما كاتبين عن اليمين وعن الشمال قعيد ما يلفظ من قول إلا لديه رقيب عتيد أما يستحي أحدكم إذا نشرت صحيفته التي أملاها صدر نهاره كان أكثر ما فيها ليس من أمر دينه ولا دنياه وعن بعض الصحابة قال إن الرجل ليكلمني بالكلام لجوابه أشهى إلي من الماء البارد إلى الظمآن فأترك جوابه خيفة أن يكون فضولا وقال مطرف ليعظم جلال الله في قلوبكم فلا تذكروه عند مثل قول أحدكم للكلب والحمار اللهم اخزه وما أشبه ذلك واعلم أن فضول الكلام لا ينحصر بل المهم محصور في كتاب الله تعالى قال الله عز وجل لا خير في كثير من نجواهم إلا من أمر بصدقة", "english": "ʿAṭāʾ ibn Abī Rabāḥ said: \"Those before you used to detest excess of speech, and they counted as excess of speech everything beyond the Book of God, Mighty and Majestic, and the Sunnah of His Messenger, peace and blessings be upon him—or a command to what is right, or a prohibition of what is wrong, or your uttering your need concerning your livelihood which you cannot do without. Do you deny that {«عَلَيْكُمْ حَافِظِينَ كِرَامًا كَاتِبِينَ، يَعْلَمُونَ مَا تَفْعَلُونَ»} (over you are watchers, noble scribes, knowing what you do)? {«مَا يَلْفِظُ مِنْ قَوْلٍ إِلَّا لَدَيْهِ رَقِيبٌ عَتِيدٌ»} (He utters not a word but that there is with him a watcher, ready.) Is not one of you ashamed that when his scroll is unfolded—which he dictated through the early part of his day—most of what is in it pertains neither to his religion nor to his worldly affairs?\"", "book_title": "Condemnation of Anger and Hatred", "section_title_ar": "Section 660", "section_title_en": "The Book of the Etiquette of Speech — Section 660: The Second Affliction — Excess of Speech (Faḍl al-Kalām)", "section_index": 660, "anchors": "- Root: فضل (Faḍl)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Excess, surplus, that which remains beyond need; the superfluous portion.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الفضل: الزيادة عن الاقتصاد\" — increase beyond moderation; blameworthy when it exceeds the due measure, as in speech surpassing the need.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): \"مال فاضل: كثير يفضل عن القوت\" — wealth exceeding sustenance. Here applied to speech: that which exceeds the necessary portion is \"faḍl\" (excess), blameworthy by analogy.\n- Root: ابي (Abā)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Refusal, strong aversion, stubborn rejection.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الإباء: شدة الامتناع\" — intense refusal; not every refusal is abā', but abā' is the emphatic form.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"أبى الله إلا أن يكون كذا\" — God refuses but that a thing be so; used of divine will overriding human inclination.\n- Root: حدث (Ḥadath)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: New occurrence, event; that which comes to be after not being.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الحدوث: كون الشيء بعد أن لم يكن\" — origination after non-existence; al-muḥdath: that which is originated.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"نزلت به حوادث الدهر\" — calamities of time befall one. Metaphorical (Majaz): \"كان عمر محدثا\" — one whose inner discernment is inspired, as if spoken to.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الفصل بين العامل والمعمول (Interposition between Operator and Operand) — The periodic structure here interposes explanatory clauses between the governing verb and its object, as in \"واعلم أن فضول الكلام لا ينحصر بل المهم محصور\" — the scope of the first clause is restricted by the exceptive particle \"بل\" (nay), requiring the reader to hold the initial assertion in suspension until the qualification arrives.", "content_type": "dua", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on supplications, prayers, and their spiritual power, from 'Condemnation of Anger and Hatred'", "badge": "Du'a"}], "18": [{"arabic": "قال الله تعالى {ما يلفظ من قول إلا لديه رقيب عتيد} إلا أن يثني على السلعة بما فيها مما لا يعرفه المشتري ما لم يذكره كما يصفه من خفى أخلاق العبيد والجواري والدواب فلا بأس بذكر القدر الموجود منه من غير مبالغة وإطناب وليكن قصده منه أن يعرفه أخوه المسلم فيرغب فيه وتنقضي بسببه حاجته ولا ينبغي أن يحلف عليه البتة فإنه إن كان كاذبا فقد جاء باليمين الغموس وهي من الكبائر التي تذر الديار بلاقع وإن كان صادقا فقد جعل الله تعالى عرضة لأيمانه وقد أساء فيه إذ الدنيا أخس من أن يقصد ترويجها بذكر اسم الله من غير ضرورة و", "english": "One of the scholars said: \"Whoever sells his brother a commodity for a dirham, while it would not be suitable for him, had he purchased it for himself, except for five dānaqs (six-tenths of a dirham) — then he has abandoned the sincere counsel (al-nuṣḥ) commanded in transactions, and has not loved for his brother what he loves for himself.\"", "book_title": "Condemnation of Anger and Hatred", "section_title_ar": "Section 325", "section_title_en": "Section 325", "section_index": 325, "anchors": "*(Anchors established)*", "content_type": "ethics", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on purifying the character, moral diseases, and spiritual remedies, from 'Condemnation of Anger and Hatred'", "badge": "Ethics"}], "19": [{"arabic": "يدري أنه من الذين سبقت لهم من الله الحسنى وقيل في معنى قوله تعالى {وجاءت سكرة الموت بالحق} أي بالسابقة يعني أظهرتها وقال بعض السلف إنما يوزن من الأعمال خواتيمها وكان أبو الدرداء رضي الله عنه يحلف بالله ما من أحد يأمن أن يسلب إيمانه إلا سلبه وقيل من الذنوب ذنوب عقوبتها سوء الخاتمة نعوذ بالله من ذلك وقيل هي عقوبات دعوى الولاية والكرامة بالافتراء وقال بعض العارفين لو عرضت علي الشهادة عند باب الدار والموت على التوحيد", "english": "...doubt concerning this truthfulness. Likewise, God Most High said: {«But righteousness is he who believes in God and the Last Day and the angels and the Book and the prophets»} (2:177) — thus He conditioned twenty attributes, such as faithfulness to covenant and patience under hardship. Then He said Most High: {«Those are the truthful ones»} (2:177). And God Most High said: {«God will raise those who have believed among you and those who have been given knowledge, by degrees»} (58:11). And He said Most High: {«Not equal among you are those who spent before the conquest and fought»} (57:10). And He said Most High: {«They are degrees before God»} (3:163). And the Prophet, peace be upon him, said: \"Faith is naked, and its garment is God-consciousness (al-taqwā).\" And the Prophet, peace be upon him, said: \"Faith has seventy-some branches; the lowest of them is removing harm from the path.\" All this indicates the interconnection of the perfection of faith with works.", "book_title": "Foundations of Belief", "section_title_ar": "Section 104", "section_title_en": "The Revival of the Religious Sciences — Book of the Secrets of Zakāh, Section 104: On the Degrees of Faith and Its Connection to Works, and the Hidden Hypocrisy (al-Nifāq al-Khafī) That Contradicts the Perfection of Faith", "section_index": 104, "anchors": "- Root: ن ف ق (N-F-Q)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Root denotes passing away, depletion, and expenditure; also the underground burrow of the jerboa (nāfiqāʾ) with a concealed exit — the etymological basis for hypocrisy as a hidden exit from faith.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"نفق الشيء: مضى ونفد\" — theological nuance: hypocrisy (nifāq) is that which causes faith to expire and pass away, whether by sale (worldly gain) or by death (spiritual ruin).\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"نفقت الدراهم\" (coins ran out). Metaphorical: \"نفق روحه: خرج\" (his soul expired and went forth) — hence the hypocrite's faith is one whose spirit has already departed.\n- Root: أ ي م (A-Y-M)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: A woman without a husband; a man long unmarried. Al-Raghib: \"الحرب مأيمة\" — war makes women widows.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"تركوا النساء أيامى\" (they left women widowed). Metaphorical application: the one who feels secure from hypocrisy is like one who claims immunity from widowhood — a false security.\n- Root: و ف ي (W-F-Y)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Fullness, completion, fulfillment of a measure or covenant.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوافي: الذي بلغ التمام\" — the one who has reached completion. \"أوفى: إذا تمم العهد ولم ينقض حفظه\" — to fulfill the covenant without breach.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"درهم واف\" (a full-weight dirham). Metaphorical: \"أوفى على المائة\" (he surpassed a hundred) — faith's perfection (wafāʾ) is only attained by surpassing mere profession to include works and inward sincerity.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد (Restriction & Focused Predication) — governing the grammatical force of \"إنما\" constructions and conditional clauses (من كان...), where the predicate is restricted to the condition stated, as in the hadith \"أربع من كن فيه فهو منافق خالص\" — the four traits are exhaustive of the ruling.", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Foundations of Belief'", "badge": "Hadith"}, {"arabic": "فكشف عن وجهه وقال ليس كذا ولكن قولي {وجاءت سكرة الموت بالحق ذلك ما كنت منه تحيد} انظروا ثوبي هذين فاغسلوهما وكفنوني فيهما فإن الحي إلى الجديد أحوج من الميت وقالت عائشة رضي الله عنها عند موته وأبيض يستسقى الغمام بوجهه .", "english": "When Abu Bakr (may Allah Most High be pleased with him) was in the throes of death, ʿĀʾishah (may Allah be pleased with her) came and recited this verse: \"By your life, wealth avails not the youth... when the breath rattles in his chest one day, and the breast is straitened thereby.\"", "book_title": "Remembrance of Death and Afterlife", "section_title_ar": "Section 1302", "section_title_en": "The Death of Abu Bakr al-Siddiq (may Allah Most High be pleased with him)", "section_index": 1302, "anchors": "- Root: حشرج (ḥashraja)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: [Core linguistic root meaning: The rattling, gurgling sound of the death-throe in the throat; the final breath struggling in the chest.]\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): [Theological/Kalam semantic nuance: The terminal state of the soul's separation from the body, the moment of overwhelming divine decree.]\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): [Literal vs Metaphorical distinction: Literal — the physical rattle of the dying; Metaphorical — the constriction of the breast as the soul is drawn forth.]\n- Root: سكر (sakara)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: [Core linguistic root meaning: The veiling of the intellect; intoxication; the stupor that overcomes one at the onset of death.]\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): [Theological/Kalam semantic nuance: The stupor of death (sakrat al-mawt) as the removal of all veils, wherein the truth of the Hereafter becomes undeniably present.]\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): [Literal vs Metaphorical distinction: Literal — intoxication from drink; Metaphorical — the overwhelming bewilderment of the soul at death's onset.]\n- Root: ذمم (dhimam)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: [Core linguistic root meaning: The covenant of protection; the bond of safe-conduct; that which one is obligated to honor and safeguard.]\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): [Theological/Kalam semantic nuance: The divine guarantee (dhimmat Allah) extended to the believer who prays the dawn prayer — a sacred pact not to be violated.]\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): [Literal vs Metaphorical distinction: Literal — a pledge of security between men; Metaphorical — the inviolable covenant between the servant and his Lord.]\n- Root: وزن (wazana)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: [Core linguistic root meaning: To weigh; the balance; the measure of equivalence between two things.]\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): [Theological/Kalam semantic nuance: The weighing of deeds on the Day of Resurrection as the manifestation of divine justice — the scales (mawāzīn) receiving only truth or falsehood.]\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): [Literal vs Metaphorical distinction: Literal — the physical act of weighing; Metaphorical — the spiritual weighing of deeds by their conformity to truth (ḥaqq) or falsehood (bāṭil).]\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — [The particle innamā restricts the predicate exclusively to its subject, as in Abu Bakr's dictum: \"It is only that the scales of those whose scales are heavy on the Day of Resurrection are weighed down by their following the truth in this world.\"]", "content_type": "afterlife", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on the journey of the soul, death, and the Hereafter, from 'Remembrance of Death and Afterlife'", "badge": "Afterlife"}], "22": [{"arabic": "يقال للأشقياء وقد انكشف الغطاء {لقد كنت في غفلة من هذا فكشفنا عنك غطاءك فبصرك اليوم حديد} ويقال أفسخر هذا أم أنتم لا تبصرون اصلوها فاصبروا أو لا تبصروا سواء عليكم إنما تجزون ما كنتم تعملون وإليهم الإشارة بقوله تعالى {وبدا لهم من الله ما لم يكونوا يحتسبون} فأعلم العلماء وأحكم الحكماء ينكشف له عقيب الموت من العجائب والآيات ما لم يخطر قط بباله ولا اختلج به ضميره فلو لم يكن للعاقل هم وغم إلا الفكرة في خطر تلك الحال أن الحجاب عماذا يرتفع وما الذي ينكشف عنه الغطاء من", "english": "Know that the lights of inner insights (anwār al-baṣā'ir) derived from the Book of God—mighty and majestic is He—and from the Sunnah of His Messenger—may God's peace and blessings be upon him—and from the pathways of reflective consideration, make known to us the states of the dead in general and their division into the blessed and the wretched. But the specific case of Zayd and ʿAmr in particular is not disclosed at all. For if we rely upon the faith of Zayd and ʿAmr, we do not know upon what they died nor how their end was sealed for them. And if we rely upon their outward righteousness, then piety (taqwā) has its seat in the heart, and it is hidden—concealed even from the possessor of that piety himself—so how then from others? Thus there is no ruling for outward righteousness apart from inward piety. God—exalted is He—said: {«إنما يتقبل الله من المتقين»} [\"God only accepts from the God-fearing\"] (Al-Mā'idah 5:27). So the knowledge of the judgment concerning Zayd and ʿAmr is not possible except by witnessing them and witnessing what transpires upon them. And when one dies, he has transferred from the world of dominion and testimony (ʿālam al-mulk wa al-shahādah) to the world of the unseen and the kingdom (ʿālam al-ghayb wa al-malakūt), so he is not seen by the outward eye. Rather, he is seen only by another eye—that eye being created in the heart of every human being. But upon it has been placed a dense covering (ghishāwah) of his lusts and worldly preoccupations, so that he does not see with it, nor is it conceivable that he should see with it anything of the realm of the kingdom (malakūt) unless that covering is peeled away from the eye of his heart.", "book_title": "Remembrance of Death and Afterlife", "section_title_ar": "Section 1327", "section_title_en": "The Unveiling of the Veil: On the Vision of the Dead and the Reality of Dreams", "section_index": 1327, "anchors": "- Root: بصر (B-Ṣ-R)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Inner perception and discernment; the eye as an organ of sight versus the \"insight\" (baṣīrah) as the heart's eye.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Baṣīrah is the perception of the heart, a spiritual sight (ru'yah qalbiyyah) that penetrates to realities, distinct from mere ocular vision.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah) sight is of the physical eye; metaphorical (majāz) sight is the heart's illumination by which hidden truths are disclosed.\n- Root: كشف (K-Sh-F)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Removal of a covering; disclosure of what is concealed.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Kashf in the theological sense is the lifting of the veil (ghishāwah) from the heart so that the unseen realm (malakūt) becomes manifest.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: uncovering a physical object; metaphorical: revelation of meanings and hidden affairs to the inner eye.\n- Root: لوح (L-W-Ḥ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: A tablet or board upon which writing appears; also the flat bones of the body.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The Preserved Tablet (al-Lawḥ al-Maḥfūẓ) is that upon which all destinies are inscribed; its modality is unknown to us save by report.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: a wooden plank; metaphorical: the divine register of all that was and will be, not corporeal but a subtle reality.\n- Root: مثل (M-Th-L)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Likeness, similitude, example; to stand in representation of another.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Mathal is the analogical representation by which an absent or abstract reality is brought near to understanding through a present likeness.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: a resembling image; metaphorical: the parabolic example (mithāl) used to approximate what transcends sensory grasp.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Rule of Restriction and Specification (al-ḥaṣr wa al-taqyīd bi-innamā) — The particle \"innamā\" confines the predicate to the subject alone, as in \"إنما يتقبل الله من المتقين,\" restricting divine acceptance exclusively to the God-fearing, a syntactic anchor for the theological point that outward piety without inward taqwā avails nothing.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Remembrance of Death and Afterlife'", "badge": "Insight"}], "23": [{"arabic": "وأما القرين فكقوله عز وجل وقال قرينه هذا ما لدي عتيد ألقيا في جهنم كل كفار أراد به الملك الموكل به وقوله تعالى قال قرينه ربنا ما أطغيته ولكن كان أراد به الشيطان وأما الأمة فتطلق على ثمانية أوجه الأمة الجماعة كقوله تعالى وجد عليه أمة من الناس يسقون وأتباع الأنبياء كقولك عن أمة محمد صلى الله عليه وسلم ورجل جامع للخير يقتدى به كقوله تعالى إن إبراهيم كان أمة قانتا لله والأمة الدين كقوله عز وجل إنا وجدنا آباءنا على أمة والأمة الحين والزمان كقوله عز وجل إلى أمة معدودة وقوله عز وجل وادكر بعد أمة والأمة القامة يقال فلان حسن الأ", "english": "I say: The Prophet (peace be upon him) was asked about the means of deliverance from these trials, and he said: \"The Book of Allah — act by what is in it, for it is that which delivers one from them.\" The questioner repeated this three times, and he (peace be upon him) said three times: \"Learn the Book of Allah, Mighty and Glorious, and act by what is in it, for in it is deliverance.\"", "book_title": "Etiquette of Quran Recitation", "section_title_ar": "Section 233", "section_title_en": "The Book of the Etiquette of Recitation — Section 233: On Understanding the Meanings of the Qur'an and the Limits of Interpretation", "section_index": 233, "anchors": "- Root: ف-ه-م (f-h-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"الفهم: هيئة للإنسان بها يتحقق معاني ما يحسن\" — comprehension as an inherent faculty by which one ascertains meanings\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الفهم: هيئة للإنسان بها يتحقق معاني ما يحسن... وأفهمته: إذا قلت له حتى تصوره\" — comprehension is a cognitive disposition; distinction between understanding (fahm) and making understood (ifhām)\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"تقول: من لم يؤت من سوء الفهم أتى من سوء الإفهام\" — literal usage; failure of understanding often stems from failure of communication\n- Root: ف-س-ر (f-s-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"وكل شيء يعرف به تفسير الشيء ومعناه، فهو تَفْسِرَتُه\" — that by which a thing's meaning is made known\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"التفسير قد يقال فيما يختص بمفردات الألفاظ وغريبها، وفيما يختص بالتأويل\" — tafsīr concerns lexical surface; taʾwīl concerns deeper meaning\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"هذا كلام يحتاج إلى فسر وتفسير\" — literal: exposition of what is obscure\n- Root: و-ج-ل (w-j-l)\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوجل: استشعار الخوف\" — the sensing of fear; used of hearts trembling at divine mention\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"لو واجلت فلانا لوجلته: لغلبته في الوجل\" — literal sense of fear; metaphorical extension to overpowering in fear\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — the particle innamā restricts predication, essential for parsing the Prophet's saying \"innamā al-aʿmālu bi-l-niyyāt\" and analogous restrictive formulations in the ḥadīth cited herein", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Etiquette of Quran Recitation'", "badge": "Hadith"}], "26": [{"arabic": "الله ولقوله ولا تجعلوا مع الله إلها آخر فمن لم يره في كل شيء فقد رأى غيره وكل ما التفت إليه العبد سوى الله تعالى تضمن التفاته شيئا من الشرك الخفي بل التوحيد الخالص أن لا يرى في كل شيء إلا الله عز وجل العاشر التبري وأعني به أن يتبرأ من حوله وقوته والالتفات إلى نفسه بين الرضا والتزكية فإذا تلا بآيات الوعد والمدح للصالحين فلا يشهد نفسه عند ذلك بل يشهد الموقنين والصديقين فيها ويتشوف إلى أن يلحقه الله عز وجل بهم وإذا تلا آيات المقت وذم العصاة والمقصرين شهد على نفسه هناك وقدر أنه المخا", "english": "For this reason, Yūsuf ibn Asbāṭ said: \"I intend to recite the Qur'an, but when I recall what is in it, I fear abhorrence (al-maqt), so I turn instead to glorification (al-tasbīḥ) and seeking forgiveness (al-istighfār).\"", "book_title": "Condemnation of Pride and Conceit", "section_title_ar": "Section 231", "section_title_en": "** The Revival of the Religious Sciences — Section 231: On the Inward Conditions of Qur'anic Recitation (Continued)", "section_index": 231, "anchors": "**\n- Root: وجل (w-j-l)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"الوجل: استشعار الخوف\" — the stirring of fear/apprehension within\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوجل: استشعار الخوف. يقال: وجل يوجل وجلا، فهو وجل. قال تعالى: {إنما المؤمنون الذين إذا ذكر الله وجلت قلوبهم}\" — the theological state of the heart's trembling upon remembrance of God\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (Haqiqah): \"رجل وجل، وقوم وجال، وقد وجل وجلا\" — the physical/emotional state of fear. Metaphorical (Majaz): The heart's trembling as the seat of spiritual perception\n- Root: قلب (q-l-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"قلب الشيء: تصريفه وصرفه عن وجه إلى وجه\" — turning and transforming\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"قلب الشيء: تصريفه وصرفه عن وجه إلى وجه، كقلب الثوب، وقلب الإنسان\" — the heart as that which is constantly turning/transforming between states\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"قلب الشيء قلبا: حوله عن وجهه\" — physical turning. Metaphorical: \"قلب المعلم الصبيان: صرفهم إلى بيوتهم\" — the heart's turning toward or away from spiritual realities\n- Root: نفس (n-f-s)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The self, soul, spirit — that which is breathed\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"النفس: الروح\" — the soul as the animating principle; also the self as the locus of blameworthy attributes\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"شيء نفيس ومنفس\" — preciousness. Metaphorical: \"دفق نفسه أي دمه\" — the self as that which must be transcended in spiritual realization\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد (Restriction & Focused Predication) — governing the precise delimitation of spiritual states (أحوال) and their hierarchical gradation (درجات)\n\n---\n\n#", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Condemnation of Pride and Conceit'", "badge": "Insight"}], "27": [{"arabic": "كفار أراد به الملك الموكل به وقوله تعالى قال قرينه ربنا ما أطغيته ولكن كان أراد به الشيطان وأما الأمة فتطلق على ثمانية أوجه الأمة الجماعة كقوله تعالى وجد عليه أمة من الناس يسقون وأتباع الأنبياء كقولك عن أمة محمد صلى الله عليه وسلم ورجل جامع للخير يقتدى به كقوله تعالى إن إبراهيم كان أمة قانتا لله والأمة الدين كقوله عز وجل إنا وجدنا آباءنا على أمة والأمة الحين والزمان كقوله عز وجل إلى أمة معدودة وقوله عز وجل وادكر بعد أمة والأمة القامة يقال فلان حسن الأمة أي القامة وأمة رجل منفرد بدين لا يشركه فيه أحد قال صلى الل", "english": "I say: The Prophet (peace be upon him) was asked about the means of deliverance from these trials, and he said: \"The Book of Allah — act by what is in it, for it is that which delivers one from them.\" The questioner repeated this three times, and he (peace be upon him) said three times: \"Learn the Book of Allah, Mighty and Glorious, and act by what is in it, for in it is deliverance.\"", "book_title": "Etiquette of Quran Recitation", "section_title_ar": "Section 233", "section_title_en": "The Book of the Etiquette of Recitation — Section 233: On Understanding the Meanings of the Qur'an and the Limits of Interpretation", "section_index": 233, "anchors": "- Root: ف-ه-م (f-h-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"الفهم: هيئة للإنسان بها يتحقق معاني ما يحسن\" — comprehension as an inherent faculty by which one ascertains meanings\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الفهم: هيئة للإنسان بها يتحقق معاني ما يحسن... وأفهمته: إذا قلت له حتى تصوره\" — comprehension is a cognitive disposition; distinction between understanding (fahm) and making understood (ifhām)\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"تقول: من لم يؤت من سوء الفهم أتى من سوء الإفهام\" — literal usage; failure of understanding often stems from failure of communication\n- Root: ف-س-ر (f-s-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: \"وكل شيء يعرف به تفسير الشيء ومعناه، فهو تَفْسِرَتُه\" — that by which a thing's meaning is made known\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"التفسير قد يقال فيما يختص بمفردات الألفاظ وغريبها، وفيما يختص بالتأويل\" — tafsīr concerns lexical surface; taʾwīl concerns deeper meaning\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"هذا كلام يحتاج إلى فسر وتفسير\" — literal: exposition of what is obscure\n- Root: و-ج-ل (w-j-l)\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"الوجل: استشعار الخوف\" — the sensing of fear; used of hearts trembling at divine mention\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): \"لو واجلت فلانا لوجلته: لغلبته في الوجل\" — literal sense of fear; metaphorical extension to overpowering in fear\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب الحصر والتقييد بإنما (Restriction & Focused Predication) — the particle innamā restricts predication, essential for parsing the Prophet's saying \"innamā al-aʿmālu bi-l-niyyāt\" and analogous restrictive formulations in the ḥadīth cited herein", "content_type": "hadith", "topic": "Prophetic traditions and scholastic commentary by Al-Ghazali, from 'Etiquette of Quran Recitation'", "badge": "Hadith"}], "29": [{"arabic": "أيديكما وما أنا بظلام للعبيد ويأمر بردهما إلى النار فيعدو أحدهما في سلاسله حتى يقتحمها ويتلكأ الآخر ويأمر بردهما ويسألهما عن فعلهما فيقول الذي عدا إلى النار قد حذرت من وبال المعصية فلم أكن لأتعرض لسخطك ثانية ويقول الذي تلكأ حسن ظني بك كان يشعرني أن لا تردني إليها بعد ما أخرجتني منها فيأمر بهما إلى الجنة وقال رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم ينادي مناد من تحت العرش يوم القيامة يا أمة محمد أما ما كان لي قبلكم فقد وهبته لكم وبقيت التبعات فتواهبوها وادخلوا الجنة برحمتي (4) ويروى أن", "english": "He mentioned me one day, or feared me in a station (1). And the Messenger of God, may God bless him and grant him peace, said: \"When the people of the Fire are gathered in the Fire — and with them, whomsoever God wills of the people of the Qiblah — the disbelievers will say to the Muslims: 'Were you not Muslims?' They will reply: 'Yes.' Then they will say: 'What did your Islam avail you, seeing that you are with us in the Fire?' They will answer: 'We had sins, and we were seized for them.' Then God, Mighty and Glorious, will hear what they have said, and He will command that whoever was in the Fire of the people of the Qiblah be brought out, and they will be brought out. When the disbelievers see that, they will say: 'Would that we had been Muslims, so that we might be brought out as they were brought out.' Then the Messenger of God, may God bless him and grant him peace, recited: {ربما يود الذين كفروا لو كانوا مسلمين} — \"Perhaps those who disbelieve will wish that they had been Muslims\" (Q. 15:2). (2)", "book_title": "Remembrance of Death and Afterlife", "section_title_ar": "Section 1360", "section_title_en": "On the Vastness of Divine Mercy and the Intercession for the Sinful — Section 1360", "section_index": 1360, "anchors": "- Root: ذكر (dhakara)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Remembrance, recollection; also mention, invocation.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Al-dhikr is the opposite of forgetfulness (nisyān); it denotes the heart's presence with what is recalled, and the tongue's utterance thereof.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah): to recall in the heart or utter on the tongue. Metaphorical (majāz): \"dhakartuhu bi-khayr\" — to speak well of someone, i.e., to raise their mention in honor.\n- Root: خرج (kharaja)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To exit, depart, emerge from an interior to an exterior.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Al-khurūj is the departure of a thing from its place or state; used of the soul leaving the body, and of the damned leaving the Fire.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: spatial egress. Metaphorical: \"kharaja min al-amr\" — to emerge from an affair, i.e., to be free of its obligation; and \"akhrajahu Allāh min al-nār\" — divine extraction by mercy, not by merit.\n- Root: شفع (shafa'a)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: To make even, pair; intercession is making one's supplication a pair with another's need.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Al-shafā'ah is the joining of one's intercession to another's request for aid; its reality is the elevation of the intercessor's rank and the remission of the offender's burden.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: pairing. Metaphorical: intercession before a king — the intercessor \"pairs\" his own honor with the petitioner's deficiency.\n- Root: رحم (raḥima)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Tenderness of heart, compassion; al-Raḥmān is intensive — mercy as an essential attribute.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Al-raḥmah is tenderness that necessitates beneficence; when attributed to God, it is pure beneficence (tafaddul) without emotional change.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the mother's womb (raḥim) as the seat of compassion. Metaphorical: \"arḥam al-rāḥimīn\" — God's mercy as sheer gratuitous giving, exceeding any creaturely pity.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Rule of Restriction and Focused Predication (al-ḥaṣr wa al-taqyīd bi-innamā) — \"Innamā shafā'atu Rasūlillāh…\" restricts intercession to a specific class, namely those who have burdened themselves with sins, not the innocent.", "content_type": "afterlife", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on the journey of the soul, death, and the Hereafter, from 'Remembrance of Death and Afterlife'", "badge": "Afterlife"}], "37": [{"arabic": "لذكرى لمن كان له قلب أو ألقى السمع وهو شهيد} ومعنى كونه ذا قلب أن يكون قابلا للعلم فهما ثم لا تعينه القدرة على الفهم حتى يلقي السمع وهو شهيد حاضر القلب ليستقيل كل ما ألقى إليه بحسن الإصغاء والضراعة والشكر والفرح وقبول المنة فليكن المتعلم لمعلمه كأرض دمثة نالت مطرا غزيرا فتشربت جميع أجزائها وأذعنت بالكلية لقبوله ومهما أشار عليه المعلم بطريق في التعلم فليقلده وليدع رأيه فإن خطأ مرشده أنفع له من صوابه في نفسه إذ التجربة تطلع على دقائق يستغرب سماعها مع أنه يعظم نفعها فكم من مريض محرور يعالجة", "english": "I say: Knowledge is like a torrent that is hostile to the elevated place — it flows only to the low ground. Thus knowledge is not attained except through humility and the casting of one's hearing [in attentive submission]. God — exalted is He — said: {«Indeed in that is a reminder for whoever has a heart or casts his hearing while present as a witness»} (Q 50:37). And the meaning of his being \"one having a heart\" is that he should be receptive to knowledge, comprehending it. But then the capacity for understanding does not avail him until he casts his hearing while being a present witness — present of heart — so that he may receive everything that is cast to him with excellence of listening, supplication, gratitude, joy, and acceptance of the benefaction.", "book_title": "Foundations of Belief", "section_title_ar": "Section 42", "section_title_en": "The Revival of the Religious Sciences — Book I, Section 42: On the Duties of the Student (Ādāb al-Mutaʿallim)", "section_index": 42, "anchors": "- Root: ع ل م (ʿ-l-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of knowing, perceiving a thing in its reality; the mark, trace, or sign by which one is guided.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"العلم: إدراك الشيء بحقيقته\" — knowledge is the apprehension of a thing in its true reality, divided into apprehension of the essence and judgment upon it.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah): \"هو من أعلام العلم الخافقه\" — he is among the lofty banners of knowledge. Metaphorical (majāz): \"خفيت معالم الطريق\" — the waymarks of the road are hidden, i.e., the traces by which one is guided.\n- Root: س م ع (s-m-ʿ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Hearing, listening; the ear; obedience and acceptance.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Hearing with attentive presence of heart, not merely the physical sensation of sound.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: auditory perception. Metaphorical: \"ألقى السمع\" — to cast one's hearing, i.e., to listen with full attentiveness and submission.\n- Root: ق ل ب (q-l-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The heart; to turn, overturn, or transform a thing from one state to another.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"قلب الشيء: تصريفه وصرفه عن وجه إلى وجه\" — the turning of a thing from one face to another; the heart as that which is ever-turning between states.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"قلب الشيء قلبا: حوله عن وجهه\" — to turn a thing from its face. Metaphorical: \"رجل قلب حول: يقلب الأمور ويحتال\" — a man who turns matters over and devises means.\n- Root: ب ح ر (b-ḥ-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The sea; vast expanse of water.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"أصل البحر: كل مكان واسع جامع للماء الكثير\" — the sea is every vast place gathering much water; then metaphorically applied to one who expands deeply into a domain.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Metaphorical: \"تبحر في العلم واستبحر فيه\" — he plunged deep into knowledge and expanded therein like a sea.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Bāb al-ḥaṣr wa-l-taqyīd bi-innamā (Restriction and Focused Predication) — the particle innamā restricts the predicate to the subject alone, negating all else; applied here to the exclusive path of the student's submission to the teacher's direction.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Foundations of Belief'", "badge": "Insight"}, {"arabic": "فلا ينال العلم إلا بالتواضع وإلقاء السمع قال الله تعالى {إن في ذلك لذكرى لمن كان له قلب أو ألقى السمع وهو شهيد} ومعنى كونه ذا قلب أن يكون قابلا للعلم فهما ثم لا تعينه القدرة على الفهم حتى يلقي السمع وهو شهيد حاضر القلب ليستقيل كل ما ألقى إليه بحسن الإصغاء والضراعة والشكر والفرح وقبول المنة فليكن المتعلم لمعلمه كأرض دمثة نالت مطرا غزيرا فتشربت جميع أجزائها وأذعنت بالكلية لقبوله ومهما أشار عليه المعلم بطريق في التعلم فليقلده وليدع رأيه فإن خطأ مرشده أنفع له من صوابه في نفسه إذ التجربة تطلع على دقائق يستغرب سماعها مع أنه يعظم نفعها فكم من مريض محرور يعالجة الطبي", "english": "I say: Knowledge is like a torrent that is hostile to the elevated place — it flows only to the low ground. Thus knowledge is not attained except through humility and the casting of one's hearing [in attentive submission]. God — exalted is He — said: {«Indeed in that is a reminder for whoever has a heart or casts his hearing while present as a witness»} (Q 50:37). And the meaning of his being \"one having a heart\" is that he should be receptive to knowledge, comprehending it. But then the capacity for understanding does not avail him until he casts his hearing while being a present witness — present of heart — so that he may receive everything that is cast to him with excellence of listening, supplication, gratitude, joy, and acceptance of the benefaction.", "book_title": "Book of Knowledge", "section_title_ar": "Section 42", "section_title_en": "The Revival of the Religious Sciences — Book I, Section 42: On the Duties of the Student (Ādāb al-Mutaʿallim)", "section_index": 42, "anchors": "- Root: ع ل م (ʿ-l-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Core meaning of knowing, perceiving a thing in its reality; the mark, trace, or sign by which one is guided.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"العلم: إدراك الشيء بحقيقته\" — knowledge is the apprehension of a thing in its true reality, divided into apprehension of the essence and judgment upon it.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal (ḥaqīqah): \"هو من أعلام العلم الخافقه\" — he is among the lofty banners of knowledge. Metaphorical (majāz): \"خفيت معالم الطريق\" — the waymarks of the road are hidden, i.e., the traces by which one is guided.\n- Root: س م ع (s-m-ʿ)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Hearing, listening; the ear; obedience and acceptance.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): Hearing with attentive presence of heart, not merely the physical sensation of sound.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: auditory perception. Metaphorical: \"ألقى السمع\" — to cast one's hearing, i.e., to listen with full attentiveness and submission.\n- Root: ق ل ب (q-l-b)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The heart; to turn, overturn, or transform a thing from one state to another.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"قلب الشيء: تصريفه وصرفه عن وجه إلى وجه\" — the turning of a thing from one face to another; the heart as that which is ever-turning between states.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: \"قلب الشيء قلبا: حوله عن وجهه\" — to turn a thing from its face. Metaphorical: \"رجل قلب حول: يقلب الأمور ويحتال\" — a man who turns matters over and devises means.\n- Root: ب ح ر (b-ḥ-r)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The sea; vast expanse of water.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): \"أصل البحر: كل مكان واسع جامع للماء الكثير\" — the sea is every vast place gathering much water; then metaphorically applied to one who expands deeply into a domain.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Metaphorical: \"تبحر في العلم واستبحر فيه\" — he plunged deep into knowledge and expanded therein like a sea.\n- Sibawayh Rule: Bāb al-ḥaṣr wa-l-taqyīd bi-innamā (Restriction and Focused Predication) — the particle innamā restricts the predicate to the subject alone, negating all else; applied here to the exclusive path of the student's submission to the teacher's direction.", "content_type": "spiritual", "topic": "Al-Ghazali's epistemic insights into the spiritual heart and inner faith, from 'Book of Knowledge'", "badge": "Insight"}], "39": [{"arabic": "بحمد ربك قبل طلوع الشمس وقبل الغروب ومن الليل فسبحه وأدبار السجود} وقال سبحانه {وسبح بحمد ربك حين تقوم ومن الليل فسبحه وإدبار النجوم} وقال تعالى {إن ناشئة الليل هي أشد وطأ وأقوم قيلا} وقال تعالى {ومن آناء الليل فسبح وأطراف النهار لعلك ترضى} وقال عز وجل {وأقم الصلاة طرفي النهار وزلفا من الليل أن الحسنات يذهبن السيئات} ثم انظر كيف وصف الفائزين من عباده وبماذا وصفهم فقال تعالى {أم من هو قانت}", "english": "This is what has been unveiled to those who gaze with the light of inner vision. But if you are not among its people, then look to the address of God Most High to His Messenger and derive it by the light of faith. For God Most High said to the nearest of His servants to Him and the highest of them in rank before Him: {«Indeed, for you in the day is prolonged occupation and [in the night] long travail. And remember the name of your Lord and devote yourself to Him with [complete] devotion»} (73:7-8). And He said Most High: {«And remember the name of your Lord [in prayer] morning and evening. And from [part of] the night, prostrate to Him and exalt Him a long [part of the] night»} (76:25-26). And He said Most High: {«And exalt [Allah] with praise of your Lord before the rising of the sun and before its setting, and during periods of the night [exalt Him] and after the [performance of] prostrations»} (50:39-40). And He said, Glorified be He: {«And exalt [Allah] with praise of your Lord when you arise. And during part of the night exalt Him and after the stars»} (52:48-49). And He said Most High: {«Indeed, the rising by night is more potent for directing the soul and more suitable for the recitation [of the Qur'an]»} (73:6). And He said Most High: {«And from [part of] the night, exalt Him and after the setting of the stars»} (52:49). And He said Most High: {«And during parts of the night, exalt Him and at the ends of the day, that you may be satisfied»} (20:130). And He said, Mighty and Majestic: {«And establish prayer at the two ends of the day and at the approach of the night. Indeed, good deeds do away with misdeeds»} (11:114).", "book_title": "Arrangement of Litanies", "section_title_ar": "Section 257", "section_title_en": "The Virtue of the Liturgies of Remembrance (Awrād) and the Elucidation That Perseverance Upon Them Is the Path to God Most High", "section_index": 257, "anchors": "- Root: ورد (w-r-d)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: The root denotes arriving at water, approaching a watering-place; the awrād (pl. of wird) are the appointed portions of devotional recitation assigned to fixed times, as one approaches the fountain of divine mercy.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The wird is the allocated share of worship apportioned to a specific hour; its theological nuance is the systematic distribution of remembrance (dhikr) and reflection (fikr) across the昼夜 cycle to combat the soul's innate restlessness.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the portion of water one drinks. Metaphorical: the fixed liturgical portion one \"drinks\" from the fountain of gnosis—the shift from physical quenching to spiritual saturation.\n- Root: لطف (l-ṭ-f)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Gentleness, subtlety, graciousness; to treat with kindness and refinement.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The divine subtlety (al-luṭf) by which God gently draws His servant; here, the soul's need for gentle management (talṭīf) through variety in worship, lest it weary.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: physical gentleness of touch. Metaphorical: the strategic, compassionate variation of devotional acts to sustain the soul's momentum—a haqīqah of spiritual governance.\n- Root: سأم (s-'-m)\n  * Lisan / Ayn: Weariness, boredom, satiety that breeds aversion; the soul's constitutional fatigue with a single monotonous mode.\n  * Al-Raghib (Mufradat): The psychological impediment to perseverance; overcome only by the divine mercy of variety, as the Prophet said: «إن الله لا يمل حتى تملوا» — God does not weary, but He has ordained that you may.\n  * Al-Zamakhshari (Asas): Literal: the nausea of over-satiation. Metaphorical: the spiritual listlessness that overtakes the heart when devotion becomes mechanical—requiring the \"turn\" (tanqul) between genres of worship.\n- Sibawayh Rule: باب التنويع والتقسيم (The Chapter of Division and Distribution) — The periodic structure here distributes predicates across multiple conditional clauses (fa-in... fa-in...), each governing a distinct outcome, requiring the translator to preserve the balanced, correlative syntax of proportional reward.", "content_type": "dua", "topic": "Al-Ghazali on supplications, prayers, and their spiritual power, from 'Arrangement of Litanies'", "badge": "Du'a"}]}, "wordTiming": {"minshawi": {"1": [[1, 510, 2018], [2, 2018, 4936], [3, 4936, 6820]], "50:1": [[1, 510, 2018], [2, 2018, 4936], [3, 4936, 6820]], "2": [[1, 400, 796], [2, 796, 3402], [3, 3402, 3838], [4, 3838, 6177], [5, 6177, 7227], [6, 7227, 8238], [7, 8238, 9407], [8, 9407, 11102], [9, 11102, 12176], [10, 12176, 12889], [11, 12889, 14010]], "50:2": [[1, 400, 796], [2, 796, 3402], [3, 3402, 3838], [4, 3838, 6177], [5, 6177, 7227], [6, 7227, 8238], [7, 8238, 9407], [8, 9407, 11102], [9, 11102, 12176], [10, 12176, 12889], [11, 12889, 14010]], "3": [[1, 1150, 2330], [2, 2330, 3490], [3, 3490, 5179], [4, 5179, 6888], [5, 6888, 7698], [6, 7698, 8420], [7, 8420, 9570]], "50:3": [[1, 1150, 2330], [2, 2330, 3490], [3, 3490, 5179], [4, 5179, 6888], [5, 6888, 7698], [6, 7698, 8420], [7, 8420, 9570]], "4": [[1, 430, 960], [2, 960, 2435], [3, 2435, 3220], [4, 3220, 4164], [5, 4164, 5182], [6, 5182, 6009], [7, 6009, 7993], [8, 7993, 8799], [9, 8799, 10020]], "50:4": [[1, 430, 960], [2, 960, 2435], [3, 2435, 3220], [4, 3220, 4164], [5, 4164, 5182], [6, 5182, 6009], [7, 6009, 7993], [8, 7993, 8799], [9, 8799, 10020]], "5": [[1, 370, 742], [2, 742, 2422], [3, 2422, 3455], [4, 3455, 4553], [5, 4553, 6749], [6, 6749, 7289], [7, 7289, 8815], [8, 8815, 9411], [9, 9411, 10640]], "50:5": [[1, 370, 742], [2, 742, 2422], [3, 2422, 3455], [4, 3455, 4553], [5, 4553, 6749], [6, 6749, 7289], [7, 7289, 8815], [8, 8815, 9411], [9, 9411, 10640]], "6": [[1, 960, 1653], [2, 1653, 4219], [3, 4219, 4846], [4, 4846, 7020], [5, 7020, 7961], [6, 7961, 8555], [7, 8555, 10040], [8, 10040, 12542], [9, 12542, 13549], [10, 13549, 14308], [11, 14308, 14638], [12, 